e":232,"path":233,"stem":234,"icon":235},"Grind Jobs","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fgrind-jobs","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F02.grind-jobs","i-lucide-workflow",{"title":237,"path":238,"stem":239,"icon":174},"Project Publishing Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fproject-publishing-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F03.project-publishing-design",{"title":241,"path":242,"stem":243,"icon":33},"Sign-In Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fsign-in-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F04.sign-in-design",{"title":245,"path":246,"stem":247,"icon":248},"Development Server","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdevelopment-server","04.platform\u002F13.development-server","i-lucide-server-cog",{"title":250,"path":251,"stem":252,"icon":253},"Supply Chain Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsupply-chain-security","04.platform\u002F14.supply-chain-security","i-lucide-package-check",{"title":255,"path":256,"stem":257,"icon":258},"Enterprise License","\u002Fplatform\u002Fenterprise-license","04.platform\u002F15.enterprise-license","i-lucide-badge-check",{"id":260,"title":59,"body":261,"description":548,"extension":549,"links":550,"meta":551,"navigation":552,"path":60,"seo":553,"stem":61,"__hash__":554},"docs\u002F03.apps\u002F03.application-structure\u002F03.artifact-types.md",{"type":262,"value":263,"toc":538},"minimark",[264,273,277,280,285,309,314,325,328,332,339,343,351,355,370,373,377,380,384,392,396,404,408,411,415,420,424,429,433,436,440,445,449,457,461,464,535],[265,266,267,268,272],"p",{},"Every project in a Kinotic application contributes one or more ",[269,270,271],"strong",{},"artifacts",". An artifact is a typed output that the platform knows how to build, deploy, and manage. This page describes each artifact type and when to use it.",[274,275,84],"h2",{"id":276},"persistence",[265,278,279],{},"The most common starting point. A persistence artifact defines your domain models (entities) and gets auto-generated CRUD services in return.",[265,281,282],{},[269,283,284],{},"What you provide:",[286,287,288],"ul",{},[289,290,291,292,296,297,300,301,304,305,308],"li",{},"Entity classes decorated with ",[293,294,295],"code",{},"@Entity"," and field-level annotations like ",[293,298,299],{},"@NotNull",", ",[293,302,303],{},"@Precision",", and ",[293,306,307],{},"@AutoGeneratedId",".",[265,310,311],{},[269,312,313],{},"What the platform provides:",[286,315,316,319,322],{},[289,317,318],{},"Full CRUD operations (save, find, delete) with pagination and sorting.",[289,320,321],{},"Named queries derived from method naming conventions.",[289,323,324],{},"Automatic storage provisioning and indexing.",[265,326,327],{},"Persistence is typically the first artifact you create because it establishes the data model that the rest of the application builds on.",[274,329,331],{"id":330},"microservice","Microservice",[265,333,334,335,308],{},"A microservice artifact is long-running code that executes in a pod or VM. It publishes services that other parts of the application can discover and call through ",[336,337,338],"a",{"href":77},"service proxies",[265,340,341],{},[269,342,284],{},[286,344,345,348],{},[289,346,347],{},"TypeScript classes that implement your business logic.",[289,349,350],{},"Service interfaces that you want to expose to the rest of the application.",[265,352,353],{},[269,354,313],{},[286,356,357,360,367],{},[289,358,359],{},"Automatic service registration in the Service Directory.",[289,361,362,363,366],{},"Service discovery and zone-based routing so other projects can call your service through a ",[336,364,365],{"href":77},"service proxy"," with a simple function call.",[289,368,369],{},"Lifecycle management, scaling, and health monitoring.",[265,371,372],{},"Use a microservice when your logic goes beyond simple CRUD -- orchestrating across multiple entities, calling external APIs, or running long-lived workflows.",[274,374,376],{"id":375},"batch-job","Batch Job",[265,378,379],{},"A batch job artifact is code that runs on a schedule. It is ideal for periodic tasks like data aggregation, report generation, cleanup routines, or syncing with external systems.",[265,381,382],{},[269,383,284],{},[286,385,386,389],{},[289,387,388],{},"TypeScript code that performs the job.",[289,390,391],{},"A schedule definition (cron expression or interval).",[265,393,394],{},[269,395,313],{},[286,397,398,401],{},[289,399,400],{},"Scheduled execution with retry and failure handling.",[289,402,403],{},"Logging and observability for each run.",[274,405,407],{"id":406},"ui-component","UI Component",[265,409,410],{},"A UI component artifact produces reusable building blocks that can be shared across multiple frontends within the application. Think of it as an internal component library.",[265,412,413],{},[269,414,284],{},[286,416,417],{},[289,418,419],{},"Reusable UI components packaged for consumption by frontend projects.",[265,421,422],{},[269,423,313],{},[286,425,426],{},[289,427,428],{},"Versioning and distribution within the application.",[274,430,432],{"id":431},"frontend","Frontend",[265,434,435],{},"A frontend artifact is a complete user interface that gets deployed as a static site. It consumes services published by other projects in the application.",[265,437,438],{},[269,439,284],{},[286,441,442],{},[289,443,444],{},"A web application (built with any framework) that compiles to static assets.",[265,446,447],{},[269,448,313],{},[286,450,451,454],{},[289,452,453],{},"Static hosting with CDN distribution.",[289,455,456],{},"Automatic connection to the application's services through the Kinotic client libraries.",[274,458,460],{"id":459},"combining-artifact-types","Combining Artifact Types",[265,462,463],{},"Most real-world applications combine several artifact types. A typical setup might look like this:",[465,466,467,483],"table",{},[468,469,470],"thead",{},[471,472,473,477,480],"tr",{},[474,475,476],"th",{},"Project",[474,478,479],{},"Artifact Type",[474,481,482],{},"Role",[484,485,486,499,511,523],"tbody",{},[471,487,488,494,496],{},[489,490,491],"td",{},[293,492,493],{},"data",[489,495,84],{},[489,497,498],{},"Defines entities and provides auto-generated CRUD services",[471,500,501,506,508],{},[489,502,503],{},[293,504,505],{},"services",[489,507,331],{},[489,509,510],{},"Implements business rules that orchestrate across entities",[471,512,513,518,520],{},[489,514,515],{},[293,516,517],{},"jobs",[489,519,376],{},[489,521,522],{},"Runs nightly data aggregation",[471,524,525,530,532],{},[489,526,527],{},[293,528,529],{},"web",[489,531,432],{},[489,533,534],{},"The main user interface",[265,536,537],{},"Each project is independently developed and versioned, but they all deploy as part of the same application and communicate through the Service Directory.",{"title":539,"searchDepth":540,"depth":540,"links":541},"",2,[542,543,544,545,546,547],{"id":276,"depth":540,"text":84},{"id":330,"depth":540,"text":331},{"id":375,"depth":540,"text":376},{"id":406,"depth":540,"text":407},{"id":431,"depth":540,"text":432},{"id":459,"depth":540,"text":460},"Learn about the different artifact types a Kinotic project can contribute -- from persistence and microservices to frontends.","md",null,{},true,{"title":59,"description":548},"1BaBm8041RP9SKrZbDluR-X4vi3Hvd-ND6RsIAx_opg",[556,558],{"title":55,"path":56,"stem":57,"description":557,"children":-1},"Learn how to configure Kinotic applications and the projects that compose them.",{"title":26,"path":69,"stem":70,"description":559,"children":-1},"How services work in Kinotic and the communication patterns they support.",1793090995145]